3.4 FPGA Habits That Break on an ASIC
Many of you have designed for FPGAs, and FPGA tools are forgiving. The bitstream initializes every flip-flop of an FPGA based on static RAM (SRAM), the synthesis tool maps arrays and multiplications onto the block RAMs and multipliers of the FPGA, and clock managers and global clock buffers are available as primitives. On an ASIC, every gate comes from the standard-cell library, and the only larger blocks are the macros of the PDK, such as SRAMs and I/O cells. Table 22 lists the habits that do not carry over.



Table 22: FPGA habits that do not carry over to an ASIC










FPGA habit
On an ASIC
Instead




initial values
dropped by synthesis without a warning
a reset (Section 3.4.1)


a register output as clock
a second clock with its own tree
a clock enable (Section 3.4.2)


an inferred block RAM
flip-flops, ten times the area
an SRAM macro (Section 3.4.3)


an inferred multiplier (DSP block)
a large, slow array of standard cells
a suitable architecture (Section 7)


an internal tri-state bus
buffers that can short or float
a multiplexer (Section 3.4.4)


clock managers, PLLs, vendor primitives
not in the cell library
analog IP, clock gating (Section 7)


the global reset at configuration
no automatic reset
a reset pin and synchronizer (Section 8)







3.4.1 Initial Values
On an FPGA, a register gets its start value with an initial value in the declaration, and the bitstream loads it at configuration. The LED blinker of the following exercise is written this way:
logic [COUNT_WIDTH-1:0] count_q    = '0;
logic                   slow_clk_q = 1'b0;
logic                   led_q      = 1'b0;
The RTL simulation honors the initial values, and the counter starts at zero in Icarus Verilog and in Verilator. Verilator flags them with -Wall, as step 1 of the following exercise shows. Yosys keeps the initial values through the generic synthesis, but an ASIC flip-flop has no way to implement them, and the mapping onto SG13CMOS5L cells drops them without a single warning in the log. In a gate-level simulation of the blinker with DIV = 4, the counter therefore stays unknown, and led_o is still x after 40 clock cycles, in which it should have toggled five times. On the chip, the registers start with random values. An initial block is for testbenches only (Table 14). In RTL, the reset sets the start values.


3.4.2 Clock Dividers and Clock Enables
On an FPGA, it is common to divide a clock with a register and to clock another register with its output, as in Figure 12 (a). On an ASIC, such a divided clock causes several problems:

A second clock. All registers clocked by slow_clk_q run on a clock of their own. It is derived from clk_i, so the paths between the two clocks are synchronous and can be timed, but each of them must be timed with the delay between the two clock edges.
A second clock tree. Clock tree synthesis (Section 10) builds a balanced tree for every clock. The divided clock arrives later than clk_i by the clock-to-output delay of the divider and the delay of its own tree, so the two clocks are skewed, which leads to hold violations on the paths between them unless the trees are balanced against each other.
Constraints. Static timing analysis only knows the clocks it is told about. A register output used as a clock needs a generated clock in the constraints (Section 6), otherwise the paths it clocks are not analyzed at all.
Test. The scan test must control every clock in the design (Section 9).
Simulation. Without a reset or an initial value, the divider starts as x and never produces an edge, so the registers behind it never change.

The solution is a clock enable, Figure 12 (b). Every rising edge of the divided clock becomes a pulse tick that is high for one cycle of clk_i, like the strobe of the tick generator in Section 2.5.7. The register stays on clk_i and only loads a new value while tick is high, otherwise it keeps its value (Section 2.5.6). The design keeps one clock, one clock tree, and one set of constraints. Where a block really needs a lower clock frequency, its clock comes from a dedicated clock generation block and is declared in the constraints. To save power, the clock of an idle block is switched off with a clock gating cell instead (Section 7). Neither of them is a register output that happens to toggle somewhere in the logic.








Figure 12: A divided clock against a clock enable, for a register that toggles at a lower rate. (a) The divider output slow_clk_q clocks the register: the design now has two clocks, and slow_clk_q needs its own clock tree and constraints. (b) Every rising edge of the old divided clock becomes a one-cycle pulse tick, which enables the register. All registers stay on clk_i.











TipExercise: An LED Blinker from FPGA to ASIC



The file content/modeling/code/led_blink/exercise/led_blink_fpga.sv is an LED blinker written with FPGA habits:


led_blink/exercise/led_blink_fpga.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: LED blinker written with FPGA habits. With a 50 MHz
// clock and the default DIV, slow_clk_q runs at 2 Hz and led_o blinks
// at 1 Hz. Works on an FPGA, but is not ready for an ASIC.

`default_nettype none

module led_blink_fpga #(
  parameter int unsigned DIV = 12_500_000
) (
  input  logic clk_i,
  output logic led_o
);

  localparam int unsigned      COUNT_WIDTH = $clog2(DIV);
  localparam [COUNT_WIDTH-1:0] COUNT_LAST  = COUNT_WIDTH'(DIV - 1);

  logic [COUNT_WIDTH-1:0] count_q    = '0;
  logic                   slow_clk_q = 1'b0;
  logic                   led_q      = 1'b0;

  // Clock divider: slow_clk_q toggles every DIV clock cycles
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i) begin
    if (count_q == COUNT_LAST) begin
      count_q    &lt;= '0;
      slow_clk_q &lt;= ~slow_clk_q;
    end else begin
      count_q &lt;= count_q + 1'b1;
    end
  end

  // The LED toggles at every rising edge of the divided clock
  always_ff @(posedge slow_clk_q) begin
    led_q &lt;= ~led_q;
  end

  assign led_o = led_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


Change into the folder content/modeling/code/led_blink. Its rtl/ already holds the solution, so move it out of the way first with mv rtl/led_blink.sv led_blink_solution.sv, and compare at the end.

Lint the file with verilator --lint-only -Wall exercise/led_blink_fpga.sv and explain the warnings.
List all FPGA habits in the file, and explain what happens to each of them on an ASIC.
Draw the block diagram of an ASIC version led_blink with only one clock, clk_i, and an asynchronous active-low reset rst_ni. Which registers do you need, and which of them need an enable?
Implement led_blink in rtl/led_blink.sv in the two-process style, with the parameter DIV and the ports clk_i, rst_ni, and led_o. After the reset, led_o must toggle every 2*DIV clock cycles, exactly as in the FPGA version. Lint your file.
Run the cocotb testbench on both versions, in the folder testbenches/cocotb: TOPLEVEL=led_blink_fpga python3 led_blink_tb.py and python3 led_blink_tb.py. Run your version also with SIM=verilator.
Synthesize both versions with yosys -p "read_verilog -sv &lt;file&gt;; synth -top &lt;module&gt;; stat" and compare the flip-flop types. Which clock does each flip-flop use?



Before continuing, please finish the previous exercise. Once you are done, compare with the solution below.






TipSolution: An LED Blinker from FPGA to ASIC






Verilator reports PROCASSINIT three times, once for each of count_q, slow_clk_q, and led_q: the registers have initial values instead of a reset.
The file has three FPGA habits. The initial values are dropped by the ASIC synthesis, so all registers start with random values, and the gate-level simulation stays x (Section 3.4.1). slow_clk_q is a register output used as a clock, which creates a second clock with its own clock tree and constraints (Section 3.4.2). And the design has no reset, so neither the simulation nor the chip has a defined start.
The block diagram is Figure 12 (b), with the tick generator made of two registers. All three registers run on clk_i: the counter count_q, the register phase_q, which takes over the role of slow_clk_q, and led_q. count_q wraps every DIV cycles (wrap). phase_q toggles at every wrap, so the old divided clock would rise exactly when wrap comes while phase_q is low. This is the enable tick of led_q. phase_q also needs an enable (wrap), count_q does not.
The ASIC version follows below. phase_q is in fact one more bit of the counter: a counter that wraps every 2*DIV cycles would do the same job, with tick at its wrap-around.



led_blink/rtl/led_blink.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: LED blinker for an ASIC, with the same timing as
// led_blink_fpga: led_o toggles every 2*DIV clock cycles. A clock
// enable (tick) replaces the divided clock, and a reset replaces the
// initial values.

`default_nettype none

module led_blink #(
  parameter int unsigned DIV = 12_500_000
) (
  input  logic clk_i,
  input  logic rst_ni,
  output logic led_o
);

  localparam int unsigned      COUNT_WIDTH = $clog2(DIV);
  localparam [COUNT_WIDTH-1:0] COUNT_LAST  = COUNT_WIDTH'(DIV - 1);

  logic [COUNT_WIDTH-1:0] count_d, count_q;
  logic                   phase_d, phase_q;  // was slow_clk_q
  logic                   led_d, led_q;
  logic                   wrap, tick;

  // Register process: one clock, one reset for all registers
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      count_q &lt;= '0;
      phase_q &lt;= 1'b0;
      led_q   &lt;= 1'b0;
    end else begin
      count_q &lt;= count_d;
      phase_q &lt;= phase_d;
      led_q   &lt;= led_d;
    end
  end

  // wrap: every DIV clock cycles. tick: where slow_clk_q of the FPGA
  // version would rise, a one-cycle enable instead of a clock edge
  assign wrap = (count_q == COUNT_LAST);
  assign tick = wrap & ~phase_q;

  // Combinational process: defaults first, then all decisions
  always_comb begin
    count_d = count_q + 1'b1;
    phase_d = phase_q;
    led_d   = led_q;
    if (wrap) begin
      count_d = '0;
      phase_d = ~phase_q;
    end
    if (tick) begin
      led_d = ~led_q;
    end
  end

  assign led_o = led_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire



All runs end with TESTS=1 PASS=1 FAIL=0 SKIP=0. In the RTL simulation, both versions behave identically: the testbench checks that led_o toggles exactly every 2*DIV cycles, with DIV = 4.
In the FPGA version, Yosys 0.70 builds 108 generic cells with 26 flip-flops: 24 $_SDFF_PP0_ for the counter (it has recognized the wrap-around to zero as a synchronous reset), one $_DFFE_PP_ for slow_clk_q with the wrap-around as enable, and one $_DFF_P_ for led_q, which runs on posedge slow_clk_q. In the ASIC version, all 26 flip-flops run on clk_i with the reset rst_ni: 24 $_DFF_PN0_ for the counter and two $_DFFE_PN0P_ for phase_q and led_q, with their enables. The ASIC version needs 117 generic cells, a few more than the FPGA version, because the asynchronous reset keeps Yosys from merging the wrap-around to zero into the flip-flops as a synchronous reset. Without rst_ni, the same RTL needs 110 cells.






3.4.3 Memories: Flip-Flops or SRAM Macros
On an FPGA, an array with a synchronous write and read infers a block RAM:


ram_ff/rtl/ram_ff.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Single-port memory with synchronous write and read,
// written as an array. On an FPGA, this infers a block RAM. On an
// ASIC, Yosys builds it from flip-flops and multiplexers.

`default_nettype none

module ram_ff #(
  parameter int unsigned DEPTH = 256,
  parameter int unsigned WIDTH = 8
) (
  input  logic                     clk_i,
  input  logic                     we_i,
  input  logic [$clog2(DEPTH)-1:0] addr_i,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0]         wdata_i,
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0]         rdata_o
);

  logic [WIDTH-1:0] mem_q [DEPTH];
  logic [WIDTH-1:0] rdata_q;

  // No reset: the content of a memory is undefined after power-up
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i) begin
    if (we_i) begin
      mem_q[addr_i] &lt;= wdata_i;
    end
    rdata_q &lt;= mem_q[addr_i];
  end

  assign rdata_o = rdata_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


On an ASIC, Yosys builds the same RTL from standard cells: a flip-flop for every bit, a multiplexer in front of each flip-flop that keeps its value while the word is not written (the library has no flip-flop with an enable input), and a tree of multiplexers for the read port. For 256 words of 8 bits, it needs 2056 sg13cmos5l_dfrbpq_1 flip-flops (2048 for the content and 8 for the read data), 2074 sg13cmos5l_mux2_1 cells, most of them the multiplexers that keep the values, and 368 sg13cmos5l_mux4_1 cells, most of them the read tree. In total, that is 165,743 µm² of cell area, or 81 µm² per bit. The SG13CMOS5L PDK, however, also provides SRAM macros ($PDK_ROOT/ihp-sg13cmos5l/libs.ref/sg13cmos5l_sram). The single-port macro RM_IHPSG13_1P_256x8_c3_bm_bist stores the same 256 words of 8 bits in 236.8 µm × 74.1 µm = 17,547 µm², 8.6 µm² per bit, which is about 9.4 times less, even before the routing of the flip-flop version is counted. Even a memory of 32 words of 8 bits needs 20,944 µm² of flip-flops and multiplexers, more than the smallest SRAM macro of the PDK, RM_IHPSG13_1P_64x16_c2 with 1024 bits in 236.8 µm × 64.36 µm = 15,240 µm².
Use flip-flops for small memories, e.g., a first-in first-out buffer (FIFO) of a few words, and for memories with several read ports, such as the register file of a CPU with two read ports and one write port. The PDK has single-port macros (RM_IHPSG13_1P_*) and two-port macros (RM_IHPSG13_2P_*), but none with more ports. For everything larger, use an SRAM macro. It is a hard macro, like the macros of the AMS chip template: the RTL instantiates it by name, and the PDK provides its simulation model, its timing library, its abstract in the Library Exchange Format (LEF) for place and route, and its layout. Its read data appear one clock cycle after the address, as in ram_ff, but details such as the behavior when a read and a write hit the same address can differ, so check them in the simulation model before you replace an array. The macros with _bm_bist in their names also offer a bit mask for the write data and a second set of ports for a memory test, which a test controller in the design drives (Section 9).


3.4.4 Tri-States
On a circuit board, several chips share a bus through tri-state drivers. Inside an FPGA, the tools convert such a bus into multiplexers. Inside an ASIC, a tri-state bus is a real tri-state bus:


pitfalls/bus_tri.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: An internal tri-state bus with two drivers, an FPGA
// and board-level habit. Do not use this on chip, write a
// multiplexer.

`default_nettype none

module bus_tri (
  input  logic       sel_a_i,
  input  logic [7:0] a_i,
  input  logic [7:0] b_i,
  output logic [7:0] y_o
);

  tri [7:0] bus;

  assign bus = sel_a_i  ? a_i : 8'bz;  // driver A
  assign bus = !sel_a_i ? b_i : 8'bz;  // driver B
  assign y_o = bus;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


Verilator lints this file without a warning. A plain synth in Yosys 0.70 warns that “Yosys has only limited support for tri-state logic at the moment” and then stops with an error, because the output y_o is “already driven” by the other driver. LibreLane runs the extra pass tribuf and maps the drivers onto the tri-state buffers sg13cmos5l_ebufn_2 of the library, so it would build the bus. Do not use one anyway. If two enables overlap, two drivers fight, and a large current flows. If no enable is active, the bus floats, the gates that read it draw cross current, and the simulation shows x. Tri-states also complicate the timing analysis and the scan test. The lowRISC style guide forbids them: “Do not use three-state logic (Z state) to accomplish on-chip logic such as muxing” (lowRISC 2026). Write a multiplexer, here assign y_o = sel_a_i ? a_i : b_i;. Tri-states belong to the bidirectional pads of the padframe (Section 10).

3.6 Architecture Determines the Result
Logic synthesis optimizes the logic you describe, but it does not invent a new architecture. A binary counter stays a binary counter, and a shift register stays a shift register. An adder written with + becomes a fast adder only if the synthesis tool knows a fast architecture for it (Section 7). The choice of the architecture is yours, and it decides area and speed much more than the details of the code.

3.6.1 Case Study: The Exclusive Counter
An exclusive counter has eight outputs q_o[7:0], of which exactly one is high at any time (a one-hot code). Its specification:

After the asynchronous active-low reset rst_ni, q_o[0] is high.
The synchronous clear clr_i makes q_o[0] high at the next rising clock edge. It has priority over en_i.
While en_i is high, the high bit moves up by one position at every rising clock edge and wraps around from q_o[7] to q_o[0]. While en_i is low, q_o keeps its value.

Figure 14 shows two architectures for this function. The first one counts in binary with three flip-flops and decodes the count into the one-hot code:








Figure 14: Two architectures of the exclusive counter. (a) A 3-bit binary counter cnt_q and a decoder: few flip-flops, but the outputs come from combinational logic. (b) A one-hot ring of eight flip-flops: each output comes directly from a flip-flop. All flip-flops run on clk_i with the reset rst_ni. In (b), the multiplexers in front of the D inputs that implement clr_i and en_i are not drawn.







excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_bin.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Exclusive counter, architecture 1: a 3-bit binary
// counter and a decoder. Exactly one of the eight bits of q_o is
// high. clr_i (synchronous, priority over en_i) and the reset select
// q_o[0], en_i moves the high bit up by one and wraps from q_o[7] to
// q_o[0].

`default_nettype none

module excl_cnt_bin (
  input  logic       clk_i,
  input  logic       rst_ni,
  input  logic       clr_i,
  input  logic       en_i,
  output logic [7:0] q_o
);

  logic [2:0] cnt_d, cnt_q;

  // Register process
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      cnt_q &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      cnt_q &lt;= cnt_d;
    end
  end

  // Combinational process: the 3-bit counter wraps by itself
  always_comb begin
    cnt_d = cnt_q;
    if (clr_i) begin
      cnt_d = '0;
    end else if (en_i) begin
      cnt_d = cnt_q + 3'd1;
    end
  end

  // Decoder: the outputs come from combinational logic
  assign q_o = 8'b0000_0001 &lt;&lt; cnt_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


The second one stores the one-hot code directly in a ring of eight flip-flops and rotates it:


excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_ring.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Exclusive counter, architecture 2: a one-hot ring of
// eight flip-flops, same behavior as excl_cnt_bin. clr_i
// (synchronous, priority over en_i) and the reset select q_o[0], en_i
// rotates the high bit up by one and wraps from q_o[7] to q_o[0].

`default_nettype none

module excl_cnt_ring (
  input  logic       clk_i,
  input  logic       rst_ni,
  input  logic       clr_i,
  input  logic       en_i,
  output logic [7:0] q_o
);

  logic [7:0] q_d, q_q;

  // Register process: the reset value is the first one-hot code
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      q_q &lt;= 8'b0000_0001;
    end else begin
      q_q &lt;= q_d;
    end
  end

  // Combinational process: rotate left by one bit
  always_comb begin
    q_d = q_q;
    if (clr_i) begin
      q_d = 8'b0000_0001;
    end else if (en_i) begin
      q_d = {q_q[6:0], q_q[7]};
    end
  end

  // The outputs come directly from the flip-flops
  assign q_o = q_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


The cocotb testbench excl_cnt/testbenches/cocotb/excl_cnt_tb.py checks the reset value, a full count with wrap-around, and 500 cycles of random clr_i and en_i against a model, and both architectures pass. To compare them, each one is synthesized onto SG13CMOS5L cells with the Yosys command of the exercise in Section 3.3.1, and the netlist is analyzed with the static timing analyzer OpenSTA, using the script excl_cnt/syn/sta.tcl. It assumes a 100 MHz clock without a clock tree, inputs that change at the clock edge, and a load of 10 fF at every output. Section 6 explains these timing constraints. Table 23 lists the results.



Table 23: The two architectures of the exclusive counter on SG13CMOS5L (typical corner, 10 fF output load, ideal clock)






