The ring needs 79 % more area. Flip-flops are expensive: a sg13cmos5l_dfrbpq_1 has 49.0 µm², a 3-input NOR gate sg13cmos5l_nor3_1 only 9.1 µm². The eight flip-flops of the ring take 392 of its 553 µm².
The ring is faster. Its outputs come directly from the flip-flops, so the path from the clock to an output is just the clock-to-output delay of 0.23 ns. In the binary version, the decoder adds two gates, and the incrementer in front of the flip-flops makes the path between them longer as well.
The ring has clean outputs. Outputs from flip-flops are free of glitches. The decoder outputs of the binary version can glitch when several counter bits change at once, e.g., from 011 to 100. This matters when q_o leaves the macro, drives a pad, or selects something in another clock domain (Section 3.5).
The binary version is more robust. Its outputs are one-hot by construction, whatever the counter holds. The ring is one-hot only as long as its state is: if it starts without a reset, or if a bit flips, e.g., from radiation, the ring can hold no high bit or several of them until the next clr_i. For the ring, the reset is mandatory.

Neither architecture is better in general: the requirements decide. Note what did not matter here: the coding style. The file excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_ring_for.sv describes the ring with a for loop instead of the concatenation, and writes the clear last, so that it overrides the rotation instead of being checked first. Yosys synthesizes it into exactly the same 28 cells with 553.2 µm². What synthesis sees is the function of every register, not the syntax that describes it.






TipExercise: A Third Architecture, the Johnson Counter



A Johnson counter (twisted ring counter) of four flip-flops s_q[3:0] shifts left and feeds the inverted most significant bit back into the least significant bit: s_d = {s_q[2:0], ~s_q[3]}. As an exclusive counter, the reset and clr_i load 0000, and the k-th state of the sequence (counted from 0) drives q_o[k]. Change into the folder content/modeling/code of the course repository. The folder excl_cnt/rtl already holds the solution, so move it out of the way first with mv excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_johnson.sv excl_cnt_johnson_solution.sv.

Starting from 0000, write down the sequence of its states. After how many steps does it repeat? Draw the block diagram of an exclusive counter built from a Johnson counter and a decoder, in the style of Figure 14.
In the sequence, every state differs from the next one in exactly one bit. Show that two neighboring bits of the ring identify every state (s_q[3] and s_q[0] count as neighbors too), and write down the decoder for the eight outputs with one 2-input AND gate each (inputs may be inverted).
Implement excl_cnt_johnson in excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_johnson.sv with the same ports and behavior as the other two architectures. Lint it and run the testbench in excl_cnt/testbenches/cocotb with TOPLEVEL=excl_cnt_johnson python3 excl_cnt_tb.py.
Synthesize your design onto SG13CMOS5L cells and analyze its timing, both in the folder excl_cnt:
yosys -p "read_verilog -sv rtl/excl_cnt_johnson.sv; \
  synth -top excl_cnt_johnson; \
  dfflibmap -liberty $LIB; abc -liberty $LIB; opt_clean; \
  stat -liberty $LIB; write_verilog -noattr excl_cnt_johnson.nl.v"
TOP=excl_cnt_johnson sta -no_init -exit syn/sta.tcl
Add a column to Table 23.
Rank the three architectures by area and by speed. Which one would you choose (a) for the smallest area, (b) for outputs that go to another clock domain, where a glitch can be captured as a pulse, and (c) for the highest clock frequency?



Before continuing, please finish the previous exercise. Once you are done, compare with the solution below.






TipSolution: A Third Architecture, the Johnson Counter






The sequence is 0000, 0001, 0011, 0111, 1111, 1110, 1100, 1000, and then 0000 again: four flip-flops pass through eight states. The block diagram is Figure 14 (a) with a 4-bit register s_q in place of cnt_q, a shift with the inverted feedback in place of the incrementer, and a decoder from 4 to 8 bits.
Within the sequence, the ones fill up from the right and then the zeros. A state is therefore identified by the position of its single 0-1 or 1-0 edge between two neighboring bits, or, for 0000 and 1111, by the equal bits s_q[3] and s_q[0] at both ends. Each output is one 2-input AND gate, as in the decoder of the following listing.



excl_cnt/rtl/excl_cnt_johnson.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Exclusive counter, architecture 3: a 4-bit Johnson
// counter (twisted ring) and a decoder of 2-input gates, same
// behavior as excl_cnt_bin. The Johnson counter passes through the
// eight states 0000, 0001, 0011, 0111, 1111, 1110, 1100, 1000.

`default_nettype none

module excl_cnt_johnson (
  input  logic       clk_i,
  input  logic       rst_ni,
  input  logic       clr_i,
  input  logic       en_i,
  output logic [7:0] q_o
);

  logic [3:0] s_d, s_q;

  // Register process
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      s_q &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      s_q &lt;= s_d;
    end
  end

  // Combinational process: shift left, feed back the inverted MSB
  always_comb begin
    s_d = s_q;
    if (clr_i) begin
      s_d = '0;
    end else if (en_i) begin
      s_d = {s_q[2:0], ~s_q[3]};
    end
  end

  // Decoder: every state differs from its neighbors in one bit, so
  // two adjacent bits identify it
  assign q_o[0] = ~s_q[3] & ~s_q[0];  // 0000
  assign q_o[1] =  s_q[0] & ~s_q[1];  // 0001
  assign q_o[2] =  s_q[1] & ~s_q[2];  // 0011
  assign q_o[3] =  s_q[2] & ~s_q[3];  // 0111
  assign q_o[4] =  s_q[3] &  s_q[0];  // 1111
  assign q_o[5] =  s_q[1] & ~s_q[0];  // 1110
  assign q_o[6] =  s_q[2] & ~s_q[1];  // 1100
  assign q_o[7] =  s_q[3] & ~s_q[2];  // 1000

endmodule

`default_nettype wire



Lint and all three tests pass (TESTS=3 PASS=3 FAIL=0 SKIP=0).
The Johnson counter needs 4 flip-flops and 24 cells with 368.3 µm². Its longest path from the clock to an output and its longest path between two flip-flops are both 0.35 ns.
By area, the binary version is the smallest (308.4 µm²), followed by the Johnson counter (368.3 µm²) and the ring (553.2 µm²). By speed, the ring is the fastest (0.23 ns from the clock to an output), followed by the Johnson counter (0.35 ns) and the binary version (0.49 ns). (a) The binary version is the smallest, and its outputs are one-hot by construction. (b) Only the ring has outputs that come directly from flip-flops and never glitch. The Johnson counter comes close: while it counts, only one bit of its state changes per step, and each output depends on only two bits, so a decoder of 2-input gates cannot glitch, unlike the binary decoder, where up to three bits change at once. A clear from, e.g., 0111 to 0000, however, changes three bits at once, and then the Johnson decoder can glitch as well. (c) For the highest clock frequency, the ring is the best choice. Like the ring, the Johnson counter needs its reset: its eight unused states form a second cycle of their own, e.g., 0010, 0101, 1011, in which several outputs are high, and the counter never leaves it without clr_i.

4.3 A Synchronous FIFO as Running Example
A first-in, first-out memory (FIFO) buffers data between a producer and a consumer that do not always run at the same rate. It makes a good example for verification: its interface is simple, but its behavior depends on the history of all pushes and pops, and the bugs hide in its corner cases, i.e., a full or an empty FIFO, a push and a pop in the same cycle, and the wrap-around of the pointers. Its asynchronous version, which connects two clock domains, follows in Section 8. The specification of our FIFO:

The FIFO stores up to DEPTH = 4 entries of WIDTH = 8 bits.
With push_i high, the FIFO stores data_i at the next rising clock edge, but only if it is not full. With pop_i high, it removes the oldest entry, but only if it is not empty.
A push and a pop in the same cycle are allowed, and each one follows its own rule. On a full FIFO, the pop removes an entry and the push is refused. On an empty FIFO, the push stores an entry and the pop is refused.
data_o shows the oldest entry while empty_o is low (first-word fall-through). While the FIFO is empty, data_o is undefined.
full_o and empty_o show the state after the last clock edge. After the reset, the FIFO is empty.

The third rule is a design decision. Many FIFOs accept a push on a full FIFO if a pop happens in the same cycle. Ours refuses it, and the specification must say so, because the testbench checks the specification, not the RTL. The RTL follows the two-process style of Section 2.5.7:


fifo/rtl/fifo.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Synchronous FIFO with first-word fall-through: data_o
// shows the oldest entry while empty_o is low. A push is accepted
// only while the FIFO is not full, a pop only while it is not empty.

`default_nettype none

module fifo #(
  parameter int unsigned WIDTH = 8,
  parameter int unsigned DEPTH = 4  // power of two, at least 2
) (
  input  logic             clk_i,
  input  logic             rst_ni,
  input  logic             push_i,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] data_i,
  input  logic             pop_i,
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0] data_o,
  output logic             full_o,
  output logic             empty_o
);

  localparam int unsigned AW = $clog2(DEPTH);

  // One bit more than the address: the extra wrap bit tells a full
  // FIFO (same address, different wrap bit) from an empty one
  logic [AW:0]      wr_ptr_d, wr_ptr_q, rd_ptr_d, rd_ptr_q;
  logic [WIDTH-1:0] mem_q [DEPTH];
  logic             push, pop;

  // Register process
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      wr_ptr_q &lt;= '0;
      rd_ptr_q &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      wr_ptr_q &lt;= wr_ptr_d;
      rd_ptr_q &lt;= rd_ptr_d;
    end
  end

  // Storage without reset: a pop never reads an unwritten entry
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i) begin
    if (push) begin
      mem_q[wr_ptr_q[AW-1:0]] &lt;= data_i;
    end
  end

  // Combinational process
  always_comb begin
    push     = push_i && !full_o;
    pop      = pop_i && !empty_o;
    wr_ptr_d = push ? wr_ptr_q + 1'b1 : wr_ptr_q;
    rd_ptr_d = pop ? rd_ptr_q + 1'b1 : rd_ptr_q;
  end

  // Status flags and read port, decoded from the registers only
  assign empty_o = (wr_ptr_q == rd_ptr_q);
  assign full_o  = (wr_ptr_q == {~rd_ptr_q[AW], rd_ptr_q[AW-1:0]});
  assign data_o  = mem_q[rd_ptr_q[AW-1:0]];

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


The storage mem_q holds four entries, organized as a ring (Figure 15). The write pointer addresses the next free entry, and the read pointer the oldest one. Each pointer has one bit more than the address, the wrap bit, which toggles each time the pointer wraps around. The difference of the two pointers, taken modulo 8, is therefore the number of entries, from 0 to 4. With the address bits alone, a full and an empty FIFO would look the same, because both pointers address the same entry. The flags therefore compare the whole pointers: equal pointers mean empty, and equal addresses with different wrap bits mean full.








Figure 15: The storage of the FIFO with DEPTH = 4: four entries, which the pointers use as a ring, addressed by the two lower bits of the pointers. The write pointer wr points at the next free entry and the read pointer rd at the oldest entry, which data_o shows. Both pointers have a third bit, the wrap bit, which toggles each time a pointer wraps from entry 3 to entry 0. Below each panel, a pointer is written as wrap bit and address, e.g., 1 00. (a) After the reset, both pointers are equal: empty. (b) After three pushes. (c) After four pushes, both pointers address entry 0 again, and only the different wrap bits tell full from empty. (d) After three pops and one more push, the two entries wrap around the end of the storage.





As in Section 3.4.3, the storage has no reset. A pop never reads an entry that was not written, so its start value does not matter, and only the pointers are reset. The flags and data_o are decoded from the registers only, without a path from an input to an output.

5.4 Inside a Liberty File
The Liberty format (.lib), introduced by Synopsys and freely available, is the de-facto standard that describes the logic, timing, and power of every cell in plain text. The header of a library defines the units and the conditions of the characterization:
library (sg13cmos5l_stdcell_typ_1p20V_25C) {
  delay_model : table_lookup;
  capacitive_load_unit (1,pf);
  leakage_power_unit : "1pW";
  time_unit : "1ns";
  ...
  input_threshold_pct_rise : 50;
  nom_process : 1;
  nom_temperature : 25;
  nom_voltage : 1.2;
  output_threshold_pct_rise : 50;
  slew_lower_threshold_pct_rise : 20;
  slew_upper_threshold_pct_rise : 80;
  ...
Times are in ns and capacitances in pF. A delay, the propagation delay \(t_\text{pd}\) of the cell, is measured from 50 % of the input swing to 50 % of the output swing, and a slew (transition time) from 20 % to 80 % of the swing. The library also defines templates for its tables. In the template of the delay tables, index_1 is the input slew and index_2 is the total load capacitance at the output, with seven values each. Here is the inverter inv_1, shortened to the parts that matter:
cell (sg13cmos5l_inv_1) {
  area : 5.4432;
  cell_leakage_power : 63.0032;
  pin (Y) {
    direction : "output";
    function : "!(A)";
    max_capacitance : 0.3;
    timing () {
      related_pin : "A";
      timing_sense : negative_unate;
      timing_type : combinational;
      cell_rise (TIMING_DELAY_7x7ds1) {
        index_1 ("0.0186, 0.0966, 0.174, 0.3294, ...");
        index_2 ("0.001, 0.0234, 0.039, 0.0648, ...");
        values ( \
          "0.0205647, 0.0848969, 0.128137, ...", \
          ...);
      }
      rise_transition (TIMING_DELAY_7x7ds1) { ... }
      cell_fall (TIMING_DELAY_7x7ds1) { ... }
      fall_transition (TIMING_DELAY_7x7ds1) { ... }
    }
    internal_power () { ... }
  }
  pin (A) {
    direction : "input";
    max_transition : 2.5074;
    capacitance : 0.00286745;
  }
}

area is the cell area in µm², and cell_leakage_power the static power in pW. The full file also lists the leakage per input state, 82.5 pW for A = 1 and 43.5 pW for A = 0, and the cell value is their average.
function gives the Boolean function of the output. Synthesis maps logic with it, and the equivalence check of Section 5.8 reads it.
capacitance of an input pin is the load it presents to its driver, here 2.87 fF. max_capacitance is the largest load the output may drive, 0.3 pF, and max_transition the largest slew an input may see, 2.51 ns, the last slew index of the tables.
A timing group describes one timing arc from the related pin (related_pin) to the pin whose group contains it, here from the input A to the output Y. timing_type says what kind of arc it is: combinational for logic, and for a flip-flop, e.g., rising_edge from the clock to Q, setup_rising and hold_rising for the timing checks of D, and recovery_rising and removal_rising for its asynchronous reset.
timing_sense is the unateness of the arc. A positive_unate arc (AND, OR, buffer) turns a rising input into a rising output, a negative_unate arc (NAND, NOR, inverter) into a falling one, and a non_unate arc (XOR, the select input of a multiplexer, the clock of a flip-flop) can do either, depending on the other inputs.
Four 7×7 tables give the delay (cell_rise, cell_fall) and the output slew (rise_transition, fall_transition) for a rising and a falling output, and internal_power gives the energy of a switching event in the same form.

A flip-flop adds a group that defines its state, here for dfrbpq_1:
ff (IQ,IQN) {
  clear : "!RESET_B";
  clocked_on : "CLK";
  next_state : "D";
}
The internal state IQ takes the value of D at the rising edge of CLK and is cleared while RESET_B is low, and the function of the output Q is "IQ". The setup and hold times of D are tables as well, with 4×4 entries over the slews of the data and the clock signal. Section 6 uses them.

7.4 Resource Sharing
Two operations that are never needed in the same clock cycle can share one operator. Multiplexers in front of the operator then select its operands. The module share/rtl/mul_sel.sv registers four 16-bit operands and computes one of two products:
p_o &lt;= sel_q ? a_q * b_q : c_q * d_q;
As written, this line describes two multipliers with a multiplexer behind them. The module share/rtl/mul_sel_shared.sv selects the operands first and multiplies them in a single multiplier:
p_o &lt;= x * y;
...
assign x = sel_q ? a_q : c_q;
assign y = sel_q ? b_q : d_q;
Synthesized as in step 2 of the exercise above, i.e., with abc -constr $CONSTR, mul_sel needs 35,237 µm² and a minimum period of 5.25 ns, mul_sel_shared 20,282 µm² and 5.38 ns. Sharing saves 42 % of the area, and the multiplexers in front of the multiplier add 0.13 ns to the path.
Why did Yosys not share the multipliers of mul_sel itself? The command synth runs the pass share, which proves with a formal solver (a solver for Boolean satisfiability, SAT) that two operations are never active in the same cycle and then merges them. In Yosys 0.70, however, alumacc runs before share and turns each multiplication into a $macc_v2 cell, which share does not consider. synth -top mul_sel -run :fine followed by stat shows the two $macc_v2 cells. With synth -noalumacc, the two $mul cells reach share, which merges them into one, with 20,251 µm² and 5.40 ns. For adders, share considers $alu cells only with its option -aggressive. One special case works without help: opt_share merges two operators with a common operand, e.g., sel ? a + b : a + c becomes a + (sel ? b : c).
The lesson is to write the sharing into the RTL where it matters, and to check with stat what synthesis built. Sharing costs multiplexers and their delay, and it only works for operations that are really never needed at the same time. The opposite, duplicating an operator or a register to shorten a path or to reduce a large fanout, is a common timing fix.

7.5 Pipelining and Retiming
The setup condition of Section 6.4 gives a register-to-register path the minimum period \(T_\text{min} = t_\text{cq} + t_\text{logic,max} + t_\text{su} - t_\text{skew} + t_\text{unc}\). ABC can only shorten the logic between the registers. A pipeline register splits the logic into stages, each of which has a full clock period (Figure 27 (a)). With \(k\) stages of equal delay,
\[T_\text{min} = t_\text{cq} + \frac{t_\text{logic,max}}{k} + t_\text{su} - t_\text{skew} + t_\text{unc}.\]
The pipeline delivers one result per clock cycle at a higher clock frequency, i.e., a higher throughput, but every result appears \(k - 1\) cycles later than without the pipeline, i.e., the latency grows. The price is the pipeline registers, their area and their clock power (Section 7.6). The overhead \(t_\text{cq} + t_\text{su} + t_\text{unc}\) of each stage limits the gain: with the values of Table 32 and an uncertainty of 0.1 ns, no stage can be clocked with a period below about 0.43 ns.








Figure 27: (a) Pipelining: a register in the middle of the logic halves the longest path between two registers, and the operand x, which enters the second half, needs a pipeline register as well. The result appears one clock cycle later. (b) Retiming: the register R2 moves backwards across the logic block B, and the longest path shrinks from 3 to 2 ns, while the function at the outputs stays the same. The delays are examples, without the clock-to-output delay and the setup time.





The work of pipelining is in the details. Every signal that belongs to the same operation must pass the same number of registers, also an operand that is only used in a later stage, such as x in the figure, and the control signals that travel along, such as a valid flag. Every testbench and every block that uses the result must expect it later.
Retiming moves registers across combinational logic without changing the behavior at the outputs (Figure 27 (b)), e.g., to balance the stages of a pipeline that is cut at the wrong place. Retiming can change the number of registers, it must compute new reset or initial values for the registers it moves, and it breaks the matching of the registers by name in equivalence checking (Section 5.8). Commercial synthesis tools retime on request. In the open-source flow, Yosys passes flip-flops to ABC only with abc -dff (in LibreLane SYNTH_ABC_DFF, off by default), and only flip-flops without a reset: yosys -h abc lists $_DFF_?_ and $_DFFE_??_. Every default script of ABC then runs dretime, which by default retimes for minimum area, i.e., it moves registers to reduce their number, not the delay. Only with a delay target, abc -dff -D &lt;ps&gt;, does ABC add a delay-oriented retime. And abc -dff must run before dfflibmap, while the flip-flops are still generic cells. The designs of this course use flip-flops with an asynchronous reset and place their pipeline registers in the RTL.






