TipSolution: Pipeline the Adder






Between the stages, the lower sum with its carry (17 bits) and the upper halves of both operands (2 × 16 bits) need a pipeline register, 49 flip-flops in addition to the 97 of add_reg, 146 in total. The upper halves wait one cycle, so that they meet the carry of their own lower halves. The lower sum waits for the upper sum in the pipeline register.
The module of the reference solution:



adder/rtl/add_pipe.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: The adder of add_reg in two pipeline stages. Stage 1
// adds the lower halves, stage 2 the upper halves with the carry of
// stage 1. The sum appears one clock cycle later than in add_reg.

`default_nettype none

module add_pipe #(
  parameter int unsigned WIDTH = 32  // even
) (
  input  logic             clk_i,
  input  logic             rst_ni,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] a_i,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] b_i,
  output logic [WIDTH:0]   sum_o
);

  localparam int unsigned H = WIDTH / 2;

  logic [WIDTH-1:0] a_q, b_q;
  logic [H:0]       lo_q;            // lower sum and its carry
  logic [H-1:0]     a_hi_q, b_hi_q;  // upper halves, one stage later
  logic [H:0]       hi;

  // Register process: input register, pipeline register, output
  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      a_q    &lt;= '0;
      b_q    &lt;= '0;
      lo_q   &lt;= '0;
      a_hi_q &lt;= '0;
      b_hi_q &lt;= '0;
      sum_o  &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      a_q    &lt;= a_i;
      b_q    &lt;= b_i;
      lo_q   &lt;= a_q[H-1:0] + b_q[H-1:0];
      a_hi_q &lt;= a_q[WIDTH-1:H];
      b_hi_q &lt;= b_q[WIDTH-1:H];
      sum_o  &lt;= {hi, lo_q[H-1:0]};
    end
  end

  // Stage 2: the upper halves and the carry of the lower half
  assign hi = a_hi_q + b_hi_q + lo_q[H];

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


The testbench prints 1000 sums, 0 errors. Without the carry lo_q[H] in the sum of the upper halves, it reports 489 errors. The width of hi matters: as the target of an assignment, the 17-bit hi makes the addition 17 bits wide, so its carry is kept. Inside a concatenation such as {a_hi_q + b_hi_q + lo_q[H], lo_q[H-1:0]}, the sum would only be 16 bits wide.

add_pipe has 303 cells, 8852 µm², and 146 flip-flops, against 264 cells, 6448 µm², and 97 flip-flops of add_reg. Its minimum period is 2.34 ns (427 MHz) instead of 3.83 ns (261 MHz), i.e., it is 1.6 times faster for 37 % more area. The formula predicts \((3.83 - 0.43)/2 + 0.43 = 2.13\) ns. The worst path ends at lo_q[16], the carry out of the first stage, after 17 cells and 2.127 ns, while the 32-bit adder of add_reg needs 32 cells and 3.629 ns. The second stage ends after 2.102 ns, so both stages are about equally long. But each half of the adder needs more than half of the logic delay of the whole adder: 1.90 ns instead of 1.70 ns.
With the port paths, add_pipe needs 2.78 ns. The path is the release of the reset: the driving cell buf_2 of block.sdc needs 1.225 ns to drive the 146 RESET_B pins of rst_ni, and the large slew raises the recovery time to 0.458 ns. With the input delay of 1 ns and the uncertainty, this adds up to 2.78 ns. In add_reg, the same path needs only about 2.3 ns, less than the 3.83 ns between the registers, so it does not show. Pipelining shortened the paths between the registers below the reset path and added 49 loads to the reset net. In the layout flow, the reset net gets a buffer tree (Section 6.7, Section 10).

8.4 Synchronizers
A two-flip-flop synchronizer gives the first flip-flop almost a whole period to resolve (Figure 31). FF1 samples the signal from the other domain and may become metastable. FF2 samples FF1 one period later, and only FF2 drives the logic of the domain. Without logic between them, \(t_r = T - t_\text{cq0} - t_\text{su}\). A third flip-flop gives the second one the same resolution time again and multiplies the MTBF by about \(e^{(T - t_\text{cq0} - t_\text{su})/\tau}\), not by \(e^{T/\tau}\), because the clock-to-output delay of the second and the setup time of the third flip-flop take their share of the period.








Figure 31: A two-flip-flop synchronizer. The signal leaves domain A from a register, so it is free of glitches. In domain B, FF1 samples it at an arbitrary moment and may become metastable. FF2 samples FF1 one period of clk_b later, so FF1 has almost a whole period to resolve. Only the output of FF2 reaches the logic of domain B.







sync/rtl/sync_2ff.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Two-flip-flop synchronizer. Brings WIDTH independent
// single-bit signals from another clock domain into the domain of
// clk_i. Each bit may arrive one cycle later than the others, so a
// bus must change only one bit at a time (Gray code).

`default_nettype none

module sync_2ff #(
  parameter int unsigned WIDTH = 1
) (
  input  logic             clk_i,
  input  logic             rst_ni,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] d_i,   // from the other clock domain
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0] q_o
);

  logic [WIDTH-1:0] meta_q;  // may be metastable, never use it

  always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin
    if (!rst_ni) begin
      meta_q &lt;= '0;
      q_o    &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      meta_q &lt;= d_i;
      q_o    &lt;= meta_q;
    end
  end

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


A synchronizer works only under a few rules:

The signal leaves its domain from a register. Combinational logic in front of FF1 can glitch, and FF1 may capture the glitch.
Nothing but FF2 reads FF1. Its output, meta_q, may be metastable. Logic between the two flip-flops takes time away from \(t_r\).
Synchronize a signal once. Two synchronizers of the same signal may resolve differently, and their outputs then disagree for a cycle.
Keep a level stable long enough. The destination must sample it at least once, so a level should last longer than a destination period plus the setup and hold window, in practice about 1.5 periods (Cummings 2008). From a fast to a slow domain, a one-cycle pulse can fall between two edges of the slow clock.
Keep FF1 and FF2 close together in the layout, so that the wire between them takes as little time as possible. Some libraries offer synchronizer cells with a small \(\tau\), but SG13CMOS5L does not.

A pulse synchronizer solves the problem of the short pulse in the fourth rule. Each pulse in domain A toggles a level, the level crosses through a two-flip-flop synchronizer, and each change of the synchronized level becomes a one-cycle pulse in domain B:


sync/rtl/pulse_sync.sv

// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Simon Dorrer
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Description: Pulse synchronizer. A one-cycle pulse in domain A
// toggles a level, the level crosses through sync_2ff, and a change
// of the level gives a one-cycle pulse in domain B. Two pulses in A
// must be more than about 1.5 periods of clk_b_i apart.

`default_nettype none

module pulse_sync (
  input  logic clk_a_i,
  input  logic rst_a_ni,
  input  logic pulse_a_i,  // one-cycle pulses in domain A
  input  logic clk_b_i,
  input  logic rst_b_ni,
  output logic pulse_b_o   // one-cycle pulses in domain B
);

  logic tgl_a_q, tgl_b, tgl_b_q;

  // Domain A: every pulse toggles the level
  always_ff @(posedge clk_a_i or negedge rst_a_ni) begin
    if (!rst_a_ni) begin
      tgl_a_q &lt;= 1'b0;
    end else if (pulse_a_i) begin
      tgl_a_q &lt;= ~tgl_a_q;
    end
  end

  sync_2ff u_sync (
    .clk_i (clk_b_i),
    .rst_ni(rst_b_ni),
    .d_i   (tgl_a_q),
    .q_o   (tgl_b)
  );

  // Domain B: every change of the level is a pulse
  always_ff @(posedge clk_b_i or negedge rst_b_ni) begin
    if (!rst_b_ni) begin
      tgl_b_q &lt;= 1'b0;
    end else begin
      tgl_b_q &lt;= tgl_b;
    end
  end

  assign pulse_b_o = tgl_b ^ tgl_b_q;

endmodule

`default_nettype wire


Each toggled level must then obey the level rule above, so two pulses must be more than about 1.5 destination periods apart. The testbench sync/tb/tb_pulse_sync.sv sends 1000 one-cycle pulses from a 10 ns into a 14.3 ns domain, five cycles apart, i.e., 50 ns, well above 1.5 periods of the destination (about 21 ns). All 1000 arrive through pulse_sync. A plain synchronizer of the pulse itself, with an edge detector behind it, catches only 699, about 10/14.3 of them, the chance that an edge of clk_b falls inside the 10 ns pulse:
